From 98185eae29caa0ce444c552a5d413e2ff8d1f47a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zabeelbasheer <53309631+zabeelbasheer@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:25:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] feat: soft delete for audits (30-day trash, auto-purge),
 creator-or-admin only

fix: mounted sub-app lifespan never actually runs

Bigger finding than the feature itself: main.py's parent app had no
lifespan of its own, and app.mount() does not propagate a mounted
sub-app's lifespan to the parent, confirmed by booting against a
completely empty data directory, no database, no tables, no seeded
users got created. This has been true since the landing-page mount
restructuring; the app has only worked because the database already
existed on disk from before that commit. Fixed by giving main.py its
own lifespan that calls init_db() directly. Verified the fix against
both a truly fresh boot (creates everything correctly) and the real
existing database with actual test audits in it (migrates the new
columns, all 3 existing rows intact, nothing lost).

Soft delete: deleted_at/deleted_by columns added via a runtime
migration (ALTER TABLE, guarded by a column-existence check) rather
than the CREATE TABLE schema, since the table already existed in
production. Sessions older than 30 days past deletion are purged on
startup. Delete and restore are restricted to the audit's creator or
an admin, auditor and DPO can view everything but that's not the same
as being trusted to make a record disappear. New 'Recently deleted'
view on the dashboard with a restore action and days-remaining count.

Verified end to end against a real running server: wrong-user delete
correctly 403s, delete removes from the normal list and appears in
trash with the right countdown, restore reverses both.
